WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.930
I've spent the last 15 years pretty
obsessed by a single question.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:16.800
Which is, what is it that holds capable

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.270
driven people from breaking through to the
next level.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:26.540
And the answer to that question to my
great surprise is success.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:31.466
I first observed this working with
executive teams in Silicon Valley where

00:00:31.466 --> 00:00:34.983
I noticed that when they were focused on a
few things it

00:00:34.983 --> 00:00:39.828
led to success but success breaded so many
opportunities and options that

00:00:39.828 --> 00:00:44.380
that diffused the very focus that led to
success in the first place.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:50.100
And so exaggerating the point in order to
make it I found that success becomes a

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.740
catalyst for failure because it leads to
what

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:56.700
Jim Collins called the undisciplined
pursuit of more.

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:03.308
The antidote to that problem is the
disciplined pursuit of less, but better.

00:01:03.308 --> 00:01:07.833
That means exploring the very critical
things you want to pursue

00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:12.439
and being willing to then, number two,
eliminate the rest, and

00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:17.802
number three build a platform for
effortless execution, so that doing what

00:01:17.802 --> 00:01:23.038
is essential becomes the default position,
not just the rare occasion.

00:01:23.038 --> 00:01:26.246
[MUSIC].

00:01:26.246 --> 00:01:31.460
My position is that when people really get
a chance to think and have the space.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:35.030
That they can quite easily discern between
the things that are

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:38.180
essential to them, important to them, and
those things that are not.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:41.760
The problem is not our ability to discern,
it's that

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.650
we don't have the space to take the time
to discern.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:48.620
Once we have that perspective, we can
think through it.

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:52.030
So my position is that we need to develop
a routine.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:54.920
That enables that space to think.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:58.950
In a world where we have so much
information, we

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:04.230
need more time to think and process it not
less.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:08.210
And so, yeah, one CEO I interviewed for

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:12.252
the book has 2 hours on his calendar
everyday.

00:02:12.252 --> 00:02:15.832
Broken up into half hour segments so that
he has this spaced

00:02:15.832 --> 00:02:20.160
stop, to turn everything off, to think, to
see the bigger picture.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.484
I think we can all do something similar to
that.

00:02:22.484 --> 00:02:25.707
[MUSIC]

00:02:25.707 --> 00:02:29.751
The idea for many people of saying no to a
senior,

00:02:29.751 --> 00:02:35.670
leader, to even family members and so on
is so unthinkable.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:37.270
They just don't even experiment with it.

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:40.500
So they end up being a novice with no.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.290
They just want to avoid this like the
plague.

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:49.690
But what I've found is that essentialists
practice and learn and develop this skill.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:54.990
Kay Krill is the CEO of Loft and the Ann
Taylor brand.

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:58.550
And she said that years ago she was really
bad at saying no.

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:04.340
And somebody came to her mentor and said
look, you've got to learn to, to get

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:10.640
rid of all of these people and commitments
that don't mean anything to you.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.380
Because they'll rob you of the things that
really

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:14.970
do and she said that because she got that

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:17.550
feedback she did learn over the years how
to

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:22.680
say no, how to push back gracefully,
sensibly, but unapologetically.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:27.256
And now she says she's very good at it,
and it saved her so many you know,

00:03:27.256 --> 00:03:31.161
wasted hours, wasted days, wasted
commitments because she

00:03:31.161 --> 00:03:34.782
actually learned to develop a repertoire
for saying no.

00:03:34.782 --> 00:03:38.123
[MUSIC]

00:03:38.123 --> 00:03:42.893
One of the executives that I interviewed
for the book, because he was trying to

00:03:42.893 --> 00:03:48.430
be a good citizen to his new company,
ended up saying yes to almost everything.

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:53.192
And his stress was going up and the
quality of work was going down and

00:03:53.192 --> 00:03:55.743
so he started to, you know, with an

00:03:55.743 --> 00:04:00.101
experiment, saying no more often, being
more selective.

00:04:00.101 --> 00:04:02.677
And what he found was that the meaning of
his work was going

00:04:02.677 --> 00:04:05.251
up, the value of his work to the company
was going up, his

00:04:05.251 --> 00:04:07.719
stress was going down and he ended up with
one of the best

00:04:07.719 --> 00:04:12.400
performance evaluations of his career and
one of the largest bonuses of his career.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.500
In that little story there is the argument
for essentialism.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:18.193
That by focusing on the few things that
are really

00:04:18.193 --> 00:04:22.280
essential, we're actually able to make a
more valuable contribution.

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:29.250
I think becoming an essentialist is not an
easy thing.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.810
I certainly think it's a revolutionary
thing.

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:34.895
You find yourself saying no when other
people are saying yes.

00:04:34.895 --> 00:04:38.810
You're gonna be saying yes, when other
people are saying no.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:43.046
But in the end, in the final analysis,
anything less than the disciplined

00:04:43.046 --> 00:04:45.131
pursuit of the essential, will lead

00:04:45.131 --> 00:04:49.020
to the undisciplined pursuit of the
non-essential.

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:52.655
And that's a price I don't think many of
us would deliberately choose.

00:04:52.655 --> 00:05:02.609
[MUSIC]

